Alessandro Scarlatti (1660-1725) and Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky (1840-1893) were two influential composers in the history of classical music. 

Alessandro Scarlatti was an Italian Baroque composer who made significant contributions to the development of opera and chamber music. He is known for his innovative use of harmony and his compositions, which include operas, oratorios, and chamber music.

Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky, on the other hand, was a Russian composer who is widely regarded as one of the greatest composers of all time. He made significant contributions to the development of ballet, opera, and orchestral music. His compositions, such as "Swan Lake," "The Nutcracker," and "1812 Overture," are still widely performed and admired today.

