What was the Taconic orogeny and how did it affect the geology of the region between New York and Quebec, Canada?
The Taconic orogeny was a mountain building period that ended 440 million years ago, which formed a great mountain chain from eastern Canada down through the present-day Piedmont of the East coast of the United States. This event resulted in the formation of a great mountain range throughout New England and eastern Canada, and led to the accumulation of sediments that eventually eroded and spread throughout the present-day Appalachians and midcontinental North America. The Taconic orogeny also led to the formation of metamorphic rocks, such as amphibolite and mica schist, which formed a belt running down the Gaspe Peninsula to Sherbrooke in Quebec, Canada.
